## E28 Periods and extents for the newer records — QUEUED · Records · no strategy, no Codex round

Authority: owner direction 23 Oct 2026; E16 (periods from the publishers; its conventions stand),
E21 (the map's time filter), E18 (Items carry their own dates); R3 (records say what the publisher
says); OSD-D7, OSD-D11.

**Outcome.** The map's time filter and the STAC catalogue reach the datasets added since E16: a
person filtering by acquisition years finds the newer surveys and registers, and the first zoom of
every dataset the publisher locates is right.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct, main e103c4c).** 160 records; 90 carry `period`, 136 carry
`region.bbox`. The 70 without a period and 24 without a bbox are listed in
`docs/program/evidence/e28/candidates.md` (written by the supervisor from `records/`). E16's
conventions: one `period` per record, `{start, end}` as the publisher states it (ISO dates or
years), with the publisher's sentence in `notes`; registers get the register's own coverage period
(first year to today, or the stated range); per-feature dates are the outline extractor's (E18);
a dataset whose publisher states no period gets none, and the PR says so. `bbox` is
`[west, south, east, north]` WGS84, approximate, for the first zoom only; a synthetic or
unlocated dataset (SPE models, Marmousi2, UNISIM, Egg, Drogon) gets none and the record's notes
say why. E29 (outlines from the map services) holds the records named in its design 3; do not edit
those.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. E16 designs 1 to 3 stand. Each record opened on the day, the publisher's sentence quoted in
   `notes` (or `period` left absent with a short "no period stated" note when the note does not
   already say so), nothing computed, nothing inferred from file names.
2. Order: records with a `manifest_url` first (their Collections hold the files people fetch), then
   national registers, then the rest. Every record at L2 without a period is checked.
3. bbox: from the publisher's stated area or map; the 24 without are each either given one with the
   publisher's words or left with a note naming why (synthetic, global repository, unlocated).
4. Nothing else in the record changes; a changed sentence elsewhere is a defect of the lane.

**Acceptance.**
- `uv run osd validate` 160 records, 0 problems; `make check` green.
- Periods: at least 35 of the 70 gain a publisher-stated period; the PR table lists each (record,
  start, end, the publisher's sentence) and names each record left without one and why.
- bbox: each of the 24 handled (given or explained), in a second table.
- Evidence `docs/program/evidence/e28/sources.md` (page, excerpt, UTC time).

**Claim.** `records/**` (`period`, `region.bbox`, `notes` only; not E29's records),
`docs/program/evidence/e28/**`.
## E18 Items carry their own dates — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · Codex round done 23 Oct (REWORK, dispositions below; no second round) · follow-on of E03 and E04

Authority: OSD-D14 (Items need a period), OSD-D11 (record versus tool boundary), OSD-D12 and
OSD-D16 (derived outlines are committed and name their inputs); E16 charter line assigning
per-feature dates to the outline side; E16 finding in `docs/program/HANDOFF.md` (register Items carry
the collection's period).

**Outcome.** A STAC client filtering the catalogue by time gets the wellbores drilled and the
surveys shot in that window, not every feature of a register: each Item of a register collection
carries the feature's own start and end as the publisher's service states them, normalised to
RFC 3339, with the raw value kept and the provenance of each bound marked; an Item falls back to
the collection's period only when the feature states nothing usable, and every Item interval lies
inside its Collection's extent.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct, main after PR #16).** `src/osd/stac.py` passes the record's
period to every Item (`_item`, both timestamps). The outline extractor emits identifiers, name,
kind and geometry only (`src/osd/outlines/extractors.py`); `rules.yaml` `propertyName` lists select
exactly those fields, so no date reaches an outline today. Outlines are committed files regenerated
by `osd verify --outlines` (`--outline-inputs` derives without writing); the build reads the
committed files. `schema/stac-osd.json` forbids unknown `osd:` members; `schema/outline.schema.json`
closes `inputs`. The gate validates a sample of Items (OSD-D18), `make check-full` the whole tree.
External prerequisites, to verify at lane open against the live services, not facts: Sodir
wellbore and survey date fields (E16 evidence names `Entry_year` and `Entered_date`; completion and
survey start/end field names unknown), NLOG `START_DATE_DRILLING` (five `1800-01-01` values that
E16 called "likely a placeholder") and an end-date field if one exists.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. Rules select dates: for each register source `rules.yaml` gains `period_fields: {start, end}`
   naming service properties, and those names are added to that source's `propertyName` request
   list; an injected-transport test asserts the request carries them. `period_placeholders` per
   source lists values treated as absent (NLOG `1800-01-01` only if the lane confirms it on the
   live service; otherwise none).
2. Outlines carry them: the extractor writes `osd:period_start_raw` and `osd:period_end_raw`
   (the service's strings, verbatim) and the normalised `osd:period_start` / `osd:period_end`
   (RFC 3339 UTC instants: a date becomes `T00:00:00Z` for a start and `T23:59:59Z` for an end; a
   timestamp without zone is read as UTC; anything else is invalid and the normalised member is
   omitted). outline/3 (`$id` bumped; outline/2 files still validate under their own schema in the
   compatibility test) admits these four members and names the date fields in `inputs`. The two
   register outline files are regenerated with `--outlines`, their diffs reviewed and committed
   (OSD-D12).
3. Items: both bounds from the feature when both normalise and start ≤ end
   (`osd:period_source: feature`); feature start with the collection's end when the end is absent
   and start ≤ collection end (`osd:period_source: feature-start,collection-end`); otherwise the
   collection's period (`osd:period_source: collection`). `schema/stac-osd.json` gains the member
   with that closed enum (`$id` bumped, old files still validate).
4. Containment guard: an offline test asserts every Item interval lies within its Collection's
   extent for the fixture and, in `make check-full`, for the whole tree. If a real register fails
   it, that register keeps the collection period in this lane and the finding goes to a records
   lane (the Collection period is E16's, records-owned); the PR says which.
5. Collection extents stay the record's `period`; the PR reports per register: Items with own
   dates, mixed, fallback; min and max feature dates against the collection period; named Item
   examples before and after.

**Acceptance.**
- Injected-transport test: the WFS request for each register includes the date fields; removing
  them from `propertyName` fails it.
- Fixture features (distinct fixture collection period, 1990–2000): dated → own bounds, exact
  RFC 3339 strings asserted per named feature id; undated → collection bounds and `collection`;
  placeholder → treated as undated; reversed → collection and `collection`; open end → feature
  start plus collection end and the mixed provenance; start after the collection end with no end
  → collection; malformed string → raw kept, normalised omitted, collection used; timestamp without
  zone → read as UTC.
- Containment test green on the fixture; `make check-full` containment on the tree.
- Compatibility: outline/2 fixture files and STAC files without `osd:period_source` validate under
  their own schemas; the schema map carries both versions.
- Mutations against the unchanged suite: placeholder rule dropped; fallback dropped; reversed
  accepted; dates read from the record instead of the feature; raw not kept; mixed provenance
  mislabelled `feature`; containment guard dropped → each red.
- After merge: the published Sodir and NLOG trees have the named example Items with the asserted
  bounds (post-publication check in HANDOFF).

**Out of scope.** Collection periods (E16, records); Items for records without outlines; records.

**Stop rules.** A register whose service exposes no per-feature dates keeps the collection period;
the lane says so and does not scrape pages.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/outlines/rules.yaml`,
`src/osd/outlines/extractors.py`, `src/osd/outlines/*.py`, `src/osd/stac.py`, `src/osd/verify.py`
(only if the outline writer needs the date fields passed), `schema/outline.schema.json` (outline/3),
`schema/stac-osd.json` (`osd:period_source`; shared with E19's access enum: second to merge
rebases), `schema/stac/schema-map.json`, `records/outlines/sodir--factpages.geojson` and
`records/outlines/nlog--nlog.geojson` (or wherever the committed register outlines live; name at
lane open), `tests/test_outlines.py`, `tests/test_stac.py`, `tests/fixtures/outlines/**`,
`docs/program/evidence/e18/**`. Depends on E03, E04, E16 (merged).

**Evidence to inspect.** The per-register counts; two named Items before and after; the
containment result; the mutation list; the outline diff summary.

**Dispositions of the Codex round (23 Oct).** Accepted: context corrected (call path, extractor
emits fixed fields, propertyName lists, field names and the 1800 placeholder are prerequisites not
facts, no generated tree in the checkout); schemas claimed and versioned (outline/3, stac-osd
`$id`), schema map and compatibility tests; the committed register outlines claimed and regenerated
with `--outlines`; date fields added to the requests with an injected-transport test; normalisation
and invalid handling defined, raw kept; open-end and mixed provenance defined with a validity rule;
containment guard added with deferral to a records lane; acceptance rewritten with exact instants
on named ids, distinct fixture period, post-publication check, and mutations for each guard; E18
named a follow-on of E03/E04. Refuted: none.
     1	# Network verification. Never a required check: a publisher's outage must not stop merges.
     2	# Pull requests touching records: verify the changed records, comment the levels.
     3	# Dispatch on main: verify (all or some), store the results on the `verification-results`
     4	# branch (OSD-D5: no bucket, no secret), then republish the site so the map shows them.
     5	name: verify
     6	on:
     7	  workflow_dispatch:
     8	    inputs:
     9	      ids: { description: "record ids, space separated (empty = all)", required: false, default: "" }
    10	  pull_request:
    11	    paths: ["records/**", "outlines/**"]
    12	jobs:
    13	  verify:
    14	    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04
    15	    timeout-minutes: 60   # L2 hashes up to 2 GB per run, 2 s pause per host
    16	    permissions: { contents: write, pull-requests: write, actions: write, issues: write }
    17	    steps:
    18	      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    19	        with: { fetch-depth: 0 }
    20	      - uses: astral-sh/setup-uv@v3
    21	        with: { python-version: "3.12" }
    22	      - run: uv sync
    23	      - name: earlier results (so a partial run keeps the others)
    24	        run: |
    25	          if git ls-remote --exit-code origin verification-results >/dev/null; then
    26	            git fetch -q origin verification-results
    27	            mkdir -p build/verification
    28	            git archive origin/verification-results | tar -x -C build/verification
    29	          fi
    30	          mkdir -p build/verification && cp -r build/verification build/verification-before
    31	      - name: pick ids
    32	        id: ids
    33	        run: |
    34	          if [ "${{ github.event_name }}" = "pull_request" ]; then
    35	            ids=$(git diff --name-only origin/${{ github.base_ref }}...HEAD -- records | sed -n 's|^records/\(.*\)\.yaml$|\1|p' | grep -v '^_' | tr '\n' ' ')
    36	          else
    37	            ids="${{ inputs.ids }}"
    38	          fi
    39	          echo "ids=$ids" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
    40	      - run: uv run osd verify --outline-inputs ${{ steps.ids.outputs.ids }} | tee verify.txt   # L3 compares committed outlines with today's inputs (E03)
    41	      - name: levels as published (the same levels records.json carries after the republish)
    42	        run: |
    43	          uv run osd status | tee status.txt
    44	          { echo '### osd status'; echo '```'; cat status.txt; echo '```'; } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
    45	      - name: dead-link report (issues opened and closed on a dispatch on main, a dry run otherwise)
    46	        continue-on-error: true
    47	        env:
    48	          GH_TOKEN: "${{ github.token }}"
    49	          GH_REPO: "${{ github.repository }}"
    50	          EVENT: "${{ github.event_name }}"
    51	          REF: "${{ github.ref }}"
    52	          IDS: "${{ steps.ids.outputs.ids }}"
    53	          RUN_URL: "${{ github.server_url }}/${{ github.repository }}/actions/runs/${{ github.run_id }}"
    54	        run: |
    55	          # an empty file when gh fails: the report then opens and closes nothing (H1)
    56	          gh issue list --state open --label origin-dead --limit 1000 --json number,title,labels > open-issues.json || : > open-issues.json
    57	          dry=--dry-run
    58	          if [ "$EVENT" = "workflow_dispatch" ] && [ "$REF" = "refs/heads/main" ]; then dry=; fi
    59	          uv run osd report --before build/verification-before --after build/verification \
    60	            --open-issues open-issues.json --verified "${IDS:-all}" --run-url "$RUN_URL" --out report.json $dry | tee dead.txt
    61	          { echo '### dead-link report'; echo '```'; cat dead.txt; echo '```'; } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
    62	          if [ -z "$dry" ]; then
    63	            if [ "$(jq '.open | length' report.json)" -gt 0 ]; then
    64	              gh label create origin-dead --color d93f0b --description "Opened by the dead-link report: an origin fell from L1+ to dead" --force
    65	            fi
    66	            jq -c '.open[]' report.json | while read -r issue; do
    67	              gh issue create --title "$(jq -r .title <<<"$issue")" --body "$(jq -r .body <<<"$issue")" --label origin-dead
    68	            done
    69	            jq -c '.recovered[]' report.json | while read -r issue; do
    70	              gh issue close "$(jq -r .number <<<"$issue")" --comment "$(jq -r .comment <<<"$issue")"
    71	            done
    72	          fi
    73	      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
    74	        with: { name: verification, path: build/verification }
    75	      - name: comment on the pull request
    76	        if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
    77	        env: { GH_TOKEN: "${{ github.token }}" }
    78	        run: |
    79	          { echo '### Verification'; echo '```'; cat verify.txt; echo '```'; echo '### osd status'; echo '```'; cat status.txt; echo '```'
    80	            echo '### dead-link report'; echo '```'; cat dead.txt 2>/dev/null || echo 'not run'; echo '```'; } > body.md
    81	          gh pr comment ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }} --body-file body.md || true
    82	      - name: full STAC tree (OSD-D18; the gate validates a sample of Items)
    83	        if: github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
    84	        run: make check-full
    85	      - name: store results and republish
    86	        if: github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
    87	        env: { GH_TOKEN: "${{ github.token }}" }
    88	        run: |
    89	          cd build/verification
    90	          git init -q -b verification-results
    91	          git config user.name "osd-verifier"; git config user.email "osd-verifier@users.noreply.github.com"
    92	          git add -A
    93	          git commit -q -m "verification $(date -u +%FT%TZ) run ${{ github.run_id }}"
    94	          git push -f "https://x-access-token:${GH_TOKEN}@github.com/${{ github.repository }}.git" verification-results
    95	          gh workflow run pages.yml --repo ${{ github.repository }} --ref main
    90	    },
    91	    "osdu": {
    92	      "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false,
    93	      "properties": {"reference_id": {"type": "string"}, "notes": {"type": "string"}},
    94	      "description": "Identifiers in the OSDU reference data where the same dataset is carried."
    95	    },
    96	    "inputs": {
    97	      "type": "array", "minItems": 1,
    98	      "description": "The publisher's own addresses for the geometry of this dataset (register services, files, stores), each with the CRS and units as the publisher states them. Extraction rules are tool configuration (src/osd/outlines/rules.yaml, OSD-D11), never fields here.",
    99	      "items": {
   100	        "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["name", "url", "format"],
   101	        "properties": {
   102	          "name": {"type": "string", "pattern": "^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$", "description": "Unique within the record; the key of its rule in rules.yaml."},
   103	          "url": {"type": "string", "format": "uri", "description": "As the publisher serves it, e.g. a WFS GetFeature request for one layer, a file, or a store prefix."},
   104	          "format": {"type": "string", "enum": ["wfs", "csv", "segy", "mdio", "geojson", "html-geojson"],
   105	                     "description": "html-geojson: a page that carries a GeoJSON geometry the publisher labels as the extent."},
   106	          "crs": {"type": "string", "pattern": "^EPSG:[0-9]+$", "description": "The CRS the publisher declares for the coordinates. Absent means not declared: the input is refused, never guessed."},
   107	          "units": {"type": "string", "enum": ["m", "ft", "ftUS", "degree"], "description": "Coordinate units when the publisher states them outside the data."},
   108	          "stated": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 300, "description": "Where and how the publisher states the CRS and units, verbatim where short."}
   109	        }
   110	      }
   111	    },
   112	    "period": {
   113	      "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["start", "end"],
   114	      "description": "The acquisition or release interval the publisher states for the dataset, inclusive (OSD-D14: STAC Items exist only for records with a period). Unquoted YAML dates and years are read as these strings.",
   115	      "properties": {
   116	        "start": {"type": "string", "pattern": "^[0-9]{4}(-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])(-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01]))?)?$", "description": "YYYY, YYYY-MM or YYYY-MM-DD; the interval starts at the beginning of it (UTC)."},
   117	        "end": {"type": "string", "pattern": "^[0-9]{4}(-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])(-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01]))?)?$", "description": "YYYY, YYYY-MM or YYYY-MM-DD; the interval ends at the end of it (UTC)."},
   118	        "stated": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 300, "description": "Where and how the publisher states it, verbatim where short."}
   119	      }
   120	    },
